  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nVisible satellite shows clear skies for inland locations with the \nexception of some lingering high cu over the desert mountain slopes. \nRadar maintains light echos over the coastal waters, likely piddly \nshowers stuck along a stalled boundary over the abnormally warm sea \nsurface temperatures. These showers may hang just offshore through \nMonday morning before being pushed eastward as a elongated upper \nlevel trough approaches tomorrow, bringing spotty showers to much of \nthe region during the day on Monday. Low and mid-level moisture will \nbe a bit lackluster with this trough, but decent energy and the \nongoing instability associated with the warmer sea surface \ntemperatures could allow for isolated thunderstorms to develop on \nthe back end of an associated front as it pushes across Monday \nevening. While the threat will be highest over the coastal waters, \nguidance continues to spill higher CAPE values into the valleys and \neven parts of the Inland Empire, where a few thunderstorms may hold \ntogether into early Tuesday morning. The HRRR emphasizes Orange \nCounty for thunderstorms on Tuesday, while the RRFS prioritizes San \nDiego County. It's worth mentioning that the HRRR has been resolving \nthis system better the past two days.  \n\nAs the trough moves over CA on Tuesday, snow levels are expected to \nfall from around 8500ft Monday afternoon to around 6000-6500ft. \nWhile model guidance has trended slightly warmer with this upcoming \nsystem, a light rain/snow mix may be observed down to 6000ft Tuesday \nmorning as the last of the precipitation comes to an end. With the \nwarmer trend, snow accumulation totals have decreased as well, and \nonly a dusting is expected for the highest terrain. Either way, \ncooler surface temperatures and gusty winds will accompany this \ntrough, with highs falling to the 60s for most locations west of the \nmountains, 30s to 40 for the mountains, 50s for the High Desert, and \n60s to 70s for the low deserts. Winds will gust to around 25-30 mph \nin the deserts, 35-40 in the mountains, and up to 45-50 mph in wind-\nprone mountain passes. Strong winds over the water will create \nhazardous marine conditions on Tuesday as well, discussed below in \nthe marine section.  \n\nDrier air pushes in behind the aforementioned trough as it moves \ninto the Great Basin on Wednesday, and zonal flow aloft sets up over \nCalifornia ahead of the next system. Temperatures won't rebound much \nif at all on Wednesday, staying 5-15 degrees below normal. The next \nsystem, a deepening low, approaches the Oregon coastline on \nThursday. Unfortunately, much of the consensus between the global \nmodels yesterday has fallen off, with less confidence today on how \nfar north the system will stay as it moves through. The trends the \npast 24 hours have pushed for a warmer and wetter system, which \ncould potentially bring very heavy rainfall for our region, \nespecially the mountains. This would also mean that there is a lower \nchance for snow accumulations in the higher terrain, with snow \nlevels on Thursday/Friday now closer to 5500-6000ft instead of the \n4500-5000ft forecasted previously. While we keep a close eye on this \nnext larger system, confidence is slowly increasing in at least some \ndecent rainfall, along with an isolated to scattered thunderstorm\nthreat. Similar to the first system, expect winds to gust as the \nlow comes across and the gradient tightens, with the strongest \nwinds currently expected Thursday night into early Friday morning.",
